(window.SkillzifyLessonContent = window.SkillzifyLessonContent || {})["power-lab"] = [{"id":"i3","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Error Handling with try/except","explain":"Errors (exceptions) crash programs unless handled. Wrap risky code in try, catch specific problems with except, and use finally for cleanup that must always run. Handling specific errors beats a bare except — it keeps real bugs visible.","takeaways":["try/except catches exceptions so your program survives them.","Catch specific errors (ZeroDivisionError) before general ones.","finally always runs — perfect for cleanup."],"code":"def safe_divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return \"Cannot divide by zero!\"\n    except TypeError:\n        return \"Numbers only, please.\"\n\nprint(safe_divide(10, 2))\nprint(safe_divide(10, 0))","quiz":[{"q":"Without try/except, what happens on 10 / 0?","options":["Returns 0","Program crashes with ZeroDivisionError","Prints a warning","Retries automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"An unhandled exception terminates the program with a traceback."},{"q":"A finally block…","options":["runs only on error","always runs","never runs","runs before try"],"answer":1,"why":"finally executes whether or not an exception occurred."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"try:\n    print(int(\"abc\"))\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"bad number\")","options":["abc","bad number","0","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"int(\"abc\") raises ValueError, which the except catches."}},{"id":"i8","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Error Handling","explain":"Real programs hit real problems: missing files, bad input, network hiccups. try/except lets your code handle failure gracefully instead of crashing. else runs when nothing went wrong, and finally always runs — perfect for cleanup.","takeaways":["Put risky code in try and the recovery plan in except.","Match specific exceptions (ValueError) before broad ones.","finally always runs — use it for cleanup like closing files."],"code":"try:\n    age = int(\"twenty\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"That is not a number\")\n    age = 0\nfinally:\n    print(\"Done, age =\", age)","quiz":[{"q":"When does the except block run?","options":["Always","Only when an error occurs in try","Before the try block","Only when there is no error"],"answer":1,"why":"except runs only if the try block raises a matching exception."},{"q":"What does finally guarantee?","options":["The error is fixed","Its code runs whether or not an error happened","The program restarts","Errors are hidden"],"answer":1,"why":"finally executes no matter how the try block exits."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"try:\n    x = 10 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    x = -1\nprint(x)","options":["-1","0","Error","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dividing by zero raises ZeroDivisionError, which is caught."}},{"id":"i12","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Lambda, map, filter","explain":"Sometimes a full def is overkill — lambda creates a tiny throwaway function right where you need it. map applies a function to every item, filter keeps only the items that pass a test, and sorted with a key function sorts by anything you can compute. Together they are a compact toolkit for transforming data.","takeaways":["lambda n: n * 2 is a tiny function with no name.","map(f, items) transforms; filter(f, items) keeps matches.","sorted(items, key=...) sorts by whatever the key function returns."],"code":"nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\ndoubled = list(map(lambda n: n * 2, nums))\nprint(doubled)\nevens = list(filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, nums))\nprint(evens)","quiz":[{"q":"What is a lambda?","options":["A small anonymous function","A type of loop","A Python module","An error type"],"answer":0,"why":"lambda defines a small function inline without a name."},{"q":"What does map do?","options":["Draws a diagram","Applies a function to every item","Removes duplicates","Sorts the list"],"answer":1,"why":"map runs the given function on each item and yields the results."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"nums = [5, 2, 8]\nprint(sorted(nums, key=lambda n: -n))","options":["[2, 5, 8]","[8, 5, 2]","[5, 2, 8]","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"Sorting by -n puts the biggest number first."}},{"id":"i13","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"*args & **kwargs","explain":"*args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple, and **kwargs collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. Together they make flexible functions that accept any number of arguments.","takeaways":["*args gathers extra positional args into a tuple.","**kwargs gathers extra keyword args into a dict.","Use *args/**kwargs to forward arguments to other functions."],"code":"def total(*nums):\n    return sum(nums)\n\ndef show(**info):\n    for k, v in info.items():\n        print(k, \"=\", v)\n\nprint(total(1, 2, 3))\nshow(name=\"Ava\", age=20)","quiz":[{"q":"What type does *args collect arguments into?","options":["A tuple","A list","A dict","A set"],"answer":0,"why":"*args always packs extra positional arguments into a tuple."},{"q":"In def f(a, *args, **kwargs):, where do extra keyword arguments go?","options":["Into a","Into args","Into kwargs","They cause an error"],"answer":2,"why":"**kwargs collects any extra keyword arguments into a dictionary."},{"q":"What does f(*[1, 2]) do when calling f(a, b)?","options":["Passes the list as one argument","Unpacks the list into a and b","Raises TypeError","Passes nothing"],"answer":1,"why":"The * in a call unpacks an iterable into separate positional arguments."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def greet(*names):\n    return \"Hi \" + \" & \".join(names)\n\nprint(greet(\"Al\", \"Bo\"))","options":["Hi Al & Bo","Hi ('Al', 'Bo')","Hi Al, Bo","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"join() glues the tuple items with the separator string."}},{"id":"i19","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Exception Hierarchy & Custom Exceptions","explain":"All exceptions inherit from BaseException, and your own error types just subclass Exception. Raising a custom exception makes your code's failures self-documenting.","takeaways":["Every built-in error (ValueError, TypeError...) inherits from Exception.","class MyError(Exception): pass creates your own exception type.","Catch specific exceptions; bare except: hides bugs."],"code":"class TooYoungError(Exception):\n    pass\ndef check_age(age):\n    if age < 13:\n        raise TooYoungError(\"Must be 13 or older\")\ntry:\n    check_age(10)\nexcept TooYoungError as e:\n    print(\"Blocked:\", e)","quiz":[{"q":"What should a custom exception inherit from?","options":["Exception","object","Error","Base"],"answer":0,"why":"Subclassing Exception plugs your error into Python's normal exception handling."},{"q":"Why avoid a bare except: ?","options":["It also catches real bugs and things like KeyboardInterrupt","It is slower","It only catches ValueError","It is a syntax error"],"answer":0,"why":"A bare except swallows everything, including bugs you actually want to see."},{"q":"What is the parent of ValueError?","options":["Exception","ValueError has no parent","TypeError","object directly"],"answer":0,"why":"All normal errors derive from Exception, which derives from BaseException."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"class Oops(Exception):\n    pass\n\ntry:\n    raise Oops(\"boom\")\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"caught\", e)","options":["caught boom","boom","Uncaught Oops","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"A custom exception IS an Exception, so except Exception catches it."}},{"id":"i20","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Raising Exceptions & Assertions","explain":"raise lets you signal errors deliberately, and assert states assumptions that should never be false. Both turn silent wrongness into loud, debuggable failures.","takeaways":["raise ValueError('msg') throws an error with your message.","assert x > 0, 'msg' crashes if the condition is False.","Use raise for bad input; assert for impossible internal states."],"code":"def divide(a, b):\n    assert isinstance(b, (int, float)), \"b must be a number\"\n    if b == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Cannot divide by zero\")\n    return a / b\n\nprint(divide(10, 2))","quiz":[{"q":"What happens when assert 2 + 2 == 5 runs?","options":["AssertionError is raised","Nothing — asserts are ignored","ValueError is raised","It prints False"],"answer":0,"why":"A failed assert raises AssertionError immediately."},{"q":"When should you use raise instead of assert?","options":["Validating user input","Checking internal logic","Never — they are identical","Only in tests"],"answer":0,"why":"raise is for expected runtime errors like bad input; asserts can be disabled with -O."},{"q":"What does raise ValueError('bad') do?","options":["Throws a ValueError carrying 'bad'","Prints 'bad' and continues","Returns 'bad'","Creates a warning"],"answer":0,"why":"raise stops execution and throws the exception with your message."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def check(n):\n    assert n >= 0, \"negative!\"\n    return n * 2\n\nprint(check(5))","options":["10","AssertionError","5","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"5 >= 0 is True, so the assert passes quietly."}},{"id":"i21","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"try/except/else/finally: The Full Picture","explain":"else runs only when no exception occurred, and finally runs no matter what — perfect for cleanup. Using all four blocks makes your error handling precise instead of guessy.","takeaways":["else: runs only if the try block succeeded — put success code there.","finally: always runs, even on return or uncaught errors.","Keep try blocks small so you know exactly what might fail."],"code":"def get_num(s):\n    try:\n        n = int(s)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"not a number\")\n    else:\n        print(\"doubled:\", n * 2)\n    finally:\n        print(\"done\")\nget_num(\"21\")","quiz":[{"q":"When does the else block of try/except/else run?","options":["Only if no exception was raised","Only if an exception was raised","Always","Never"],"answer":0,"why":"else means 'the try succeeded, so do the success path here'."},{"q":"When does finally run?","options":["Always, even after return or an error","Only on success","Only on error","Only if there is no else"],"answer":0,"why":"finally is guaranteed cleanup — it runs no matter how the try exits."},{"q":"Why keep try blocks small?","options":["So you know exactly which line might fail","It runs faster","Big try blocks are a syntax error","Exceptions cannot cross function calls"],"answer":0,"why":"A tiny try block pinpoints the risky operation instead of hiding bugs elsewhere."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"try:\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    x = -1\nelse:\n    x = 99\nfinally:\n    x += 1\nprint(x)","options":["0","-1","99","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"The exception skips else; finally still adds 1 to -1."}},{"id":"i29","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Iterators & the Iterator Protocol","explain":"An iterator is an object with __next__() that hands out one item at a time; iter() gets one from any iterable. for loops are just pretty wrappers around this protocol.","takeaways":["iter([1, 2]) returns an iterator; next(it) pulls the next item.","When items run out, next() raises StopIteration.","Custom classes become iterable by defining __iter__ and __next__."],"code":"it = iter([\"a\", \"b\"])\n\nprint(next(it))\nprint(next(it))\n\nfor x in [10, 20]:\n    print(x)","quiz":[{"q":"What does next(it) do when the iterator is exhausted?","options":["Raises StopIteration","Returns None","Restarts from the beginning","Returns 0"],"answer":0,"why":"StopIteration is the signal that there are no more items — for loops catch it silently."},{"q":"What does iter([1, 2, 3]) return?","options":["An iterator over the list","The list itself","The number 1","A copy of the list"],"answer":0,"why":"iter() builds a fresh iterator object that tracks position separately from the list."},{"q":"Which two methods make a custom iterator?","options":["__iter__ and __next__","__init__ and __str__","__get__ and __set__","__call__ and __len__"],"answer":0,"why":"__iter__ returns the iterator and __next__ produces each item — that is the protocol."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"it = iter([5, 6])\nfirst = next(it)\nrest = list(it)\nprint(first)\nprint(rest)","options":["5\n[6]","[5, 6]\n5","5\n[5, 6]","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first next() consumes 5, so list() only sees what is left."}},{"id":"i30","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Generator Expressions & itertools","explain":"Generator expressions (x*2 for x in data) compute values lazily, one at a time — perfect for huge sequences. itertools adds pro tools like count(), cycle(), and chain() for combining iterables.","takeaways":["(x*x for x in range(10**9)) uses almost no memory — values are made on demand.","itertools.count(5) is an infinite counter; pair with islice to take a few.","itertools.chain(a, b) loops over a then b as one stream."],"code":"import itertools\n\ngen = (x * x for x in range(5))\nprint(list(gen))\n\ncombo = itertools.chain(\"ab\", [1, 2])\nprint(list(combo))","quiz":[{"q":"How is (x for x in data) different from [x for x in data]?","options":["It is lazy — values are produced one at a time","It is faster for small lists","There is no difference","It returns a tuple"],"answer":0,"why":"Parentheses make a generator: nothing is computed until you iterate."},{"q":"What does itertools.chain('ab', [1]) yield?","options":["'a', 'b', 1","('ab', [1])","'ab1'","Error"],"answer":0,"why":"chain flattens its arguments into one continuous stream of items."},{"q":"Why use a generator for 10**9 items?","options":["It never stores them all in memory","It runs 100x faster","Lists cannot hold that many","Generators sort automatically"],"answer":0,"why":"A generator holds only its current state, so memory stays tiny no matter the size."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import itertools\n\nc = itertools.count(10)\nprint([next(c) for _ in range(3)])","options":["[10, 11, 12]","[0, 1, 2]","Infinite loop","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"count(10) starts at 10 and steps by 1."}},{"id":"i32","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Closures & Higher-Order Functions","explain":"A closure is a function that remembers variables from the scope where it was created. Functions that take or return other functions — like decorators do — are called higher-order functions.","takeaways":["An inner function keeps access to its enclosing function's variables.","return inner (no parens) hands back the function itself.","Higher-order functions take functions as args or return them."],"code":"def make_adder(n):\n    def adder(x):\n        return x + n\n    return adder\n\nadd5 = make_adder(5)\nprint(add5(10))\nprint(add5(20))","quiz":[{"q":"What does make_adder(5) return?","options":["A function that adds 5 to its argument","15","The number 5","None"],"answer":0,"why":"It returns the inner adder function, which still remembers n=5."},{"q":"What makes adder a closure?","options":["It remembers n from make_adder's scope","It is defined with lambda","It takes two arguments","It is recursive"],"answer":0,"why":"A closure captures variables from its enclosing scope even after that scope exits."},{"q":"What is a higher-order function?","options":["One that takes or returns functions","One with many parameters","One defined inside a class","One that calls itself"],"answer":0,"why":"Treating functions as values — passing or returning them — is the definition."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def power(exp):\n    def calc(base):\n        return base ** exp\n    return calc\n\nsquare = power(2)\nprint(square(6))","options":["36","12","64","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"square remembers exp=2, so it computes 6**2."}},{"id":"i74","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Testing Your Algorithms: Writing Good Tests","explain":"An algorithm you cannot test is an algorithm you cannot trust. A good test suite checks the normal case, the edge cases (empty input, single item, duplicates), and the case that used to fail — with assert statements that fail loudly the moment something breaks.","takeaways":["Test normal cases, edge cases (empty, single item), and duplicates.","assert condition stops the run with AssertionError when it fails.","Write the test from the expected answer, not from what the code does.","One small test per behaviour beats one giant test that checks everything."],"code":"def two_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}\n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        if target - num in seen:\n            return [seen[target - num], i]\n        seen[num] = i\n    return []\n\ndef test_two_sum():\n    assert two_sum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9) == [0, 1]\n    assert two_sum([3, 2, 4], 6) == [1, 2]\n    assert two_sum([1, 2, 3], 7) == []\n    print(\"All 3 tests passed\")\n\ntest_two_sum()","quiz":[{"q":"A good test checks...","options":["Only the happy path","Only huge inputs","Only the code style","Normal cases, edge cases, and empty input"],"answer":3,"why":"Bugs hide in edges and empty inputs, not in the obvious normal case."},{"q":"What does assert do when its condition is False?","options":["Returns False","Raises AssertionError","Prints a warning","Exits silently"],"answer":1,"why":"A failed assert raises AssertionError immediately, so a broken test cannot pass quietly."},{"q":"Why test the empty-input case?","options":["It is required by Python","Edge cases are where bugs hide","It makes tests slower","It is never needed"],"answer":1,"why":"Empty input is the most common crash site — loops, indexing, and max() all misbehave there."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nassert add(2, 3) == 5\nprint(\"Test passed\")","options":["Test passed","AssertionError","5","Nothing prints"],"answer":0,"hint":"The assert holds, so the code keeps going to the print."}},{"id":"i79","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Generators for Large Data","explain":"A generator function uses yield to hand out one value at a time, so a million-item sequence costs almost no memory. You can sum it, loop over it, or feed it to another function without ever building a list.","takeaways":["`yield` turns a function into a generator that produces values lazily.","A generator holds only its current state, not the whole sequence.","Once a generator is exhausted, it yields nothing more — make a new one."],"code":"def squares(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i * i\n\nprint(sum(squares(1000)))\nprint(list(squares(5)))","quiz":[{"q":"How is a generator different from a list?","options":["A generator stores all values at once","A generator makes values one at a time, using little memory","A generator is always faster","A generator can be indexed like a list"],"answer":1,"why":"A generator computes each value on demand and never stores the whole sequence."},{"q":"What happens after you fully consume a generator?","options":["It restarts from the beginning","It is exhausted and yields nothing more","It turns into a list","It raises ValueError"],"answer":1,"why":"A generator is single-use; once it is done, iterating again gives nothing."},{"q":"Which uses the least memory for 10 million numbers?","options":["A list comprehension","A generator expression","A list built with append in a loop","They all use the same memory"],"answer":1,"why":"A generator expression like (x for x in range(10**7)) holds only one value at a time."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"g = (x * x for x in range(4))\nprint(sum(g))\nprint(sum(g))","options":["14\n14","0\n14","14\n0","Error"],"answer":2,"hint":"The first sum uses up the generator — what is left for the second?"}},{"id":"i83","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Error Handling in Real Programs","explain":"Real programs meet bad input all the time: a mistyped number, a missing file, a zero divisor. Wrapping the risky part in try/except and catching only the specific error keeps the program running and the bug visible.","takeaways":["Catch the specific error, like `ValueError` — never hide every error with a bare `except:`.","`else` runs only when the try block succeeds; `finally` always runs.","A helper that returns `None` on bad input turns crashes into data you can check."],"code":"def safe_int(text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        return None\n\nprint(safe_int(\"42\"))\nprint(safe_int(\"oops\"))\nprint(safe_int(\"7\"))\n\ntry:\n    print(10 / 0)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(\"cannot divide by zero\")","quiz":[{"q":"Why catch ValueError specifically instead of a bare except?","options":["So unrelated bugs are not silently hidden","It is shorter to type","It runs faster","It catches every error"],"answer":0,"why":"A bare except swallows real bugs too; catching one type leaves the rest visible."},{"q":"When does the else block of try/except/else run?","options":["Only when an exception happens","When no exception happens in the try block","Always, even on error","Never"],"answer":1,"why":"else is the success path — it runs only if the try block finished cleanly."},{"q":"What does safe_int(\"oops\") return?","options":["None","0","'oops'","It raises ValueError"],"answer":0,"why":"int('oops') raises ValueError, which safe_int catches and turns into None."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return 0\n\nprint(divide(10, 2))\nprint(divide(10, 0))","options":["5\n0","0\n5.0","5.0\n0","Error"],"answer":2,"hint":"10 / 2 is a float division; the second call hits the except branch."}},{"id":"i84","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Logging in Practice","explain":"print() is fine for quick checks, but real programs use the logging module: every message carries a level — DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR — and you can send logs to a file or silence the chatty ones with one setting.","takeaways":["Levels go DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR; only messages at or above the set level show.","`logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)` sets the level in one line.","`stream=sys.stdout` sends logs to standard output so they show up wherever prints do.","Logs can carry timestamps and go to a file — print() cannot do either."],"code":"import logging, sys\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\", stream=sys.stdout)\n\nlogging.info(\"Phonebook loaded\")\nlogging.warning(\"Bo has no phone number\")\nlogging.error(\"Could not save contacts\")\nlogging.debug(\"This stays hidden\")","quiz":[{"q":"What is the order of logging levels from quietest to loudest?","options":["DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR","ERROR < WARNING < INFO < DEBUG","INFO < DEBUG < ERROR < WARNING","All levels are equal"],"answer":0,"why":"DEBUG is the most detailed, ERROR the most severe."},{"q":"Why use logging instead of print() in a real program?","options":["print() cannot show text","Logs carry a level and can go to a file or include timestamps","Logging is faster to type","print() is removed in Python 3"],"answer":1,"why":"Levels let you filter noise, and handlers can write logs to a file for later."},{"q":"With level=logging.WARNING, which message shows?","options":["DEBUG","INFO","WARNING","All of them"],"answer":2,"why":"Only WARNING and above show; DEBUG and INFO are below the threshold."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import logging, sys\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.WARNING, format=\"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\", stream=sys.stdout)\nlogging.info(\"started\")\nlogging.warning(\"low disk\")","options":["INFO: started","WARNING: low disk","INFO: started\nWARNING: low disk","Nothing prints"],"answer":1,"hint":"INFO sits below the WARNING threshold, so it is filtered out."}},{"id":"i85","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Virtual Environments in Practice","explain":"A virtual environment is a private copy of Python for one project, so its packages never clash with another project's. You create it once with python -m venv, activate it, and pip installs everything inside it.","takeaways":["`python -m venv .venv` creates a private environment in a .venv folder.","Activate it, then `pip install` puts packages inside the environment only.","`pip freeze > requirements.txt` records exact versions so anyone can rebuild it."],"code":"import os, sys\n\nprint(sys.executable)\nprint(sys.prefix)\nprint(os.environ.get(\"VIRTUAL_ENV\", \"No virtual environment active\"))\nprint(sys.version.split()[0])","quiz":[{"q":"What does a virtual environment do for a project?","options":["Isolates a project's packages from other projects","Speeds up Python","Deletes system Python","Compiles your code"],"answer":0,"why":"Each venv has its own packages, so two projects can use different versions of the same library."},{"q":"Which command creates a virtual environment named .venv?","options":["pip install venv","python -m venv .venv","python --venv","venv create .venv"],"answer":1,"why":"python -m venv .venv builds the environment in a .venv folder."},{"q":"After activating a venv, where does pip install packages?","options":["Into the active environment, not system Python","Into the source code file","Into requirements.txt itself","Nowhere — pip only lists them"],"answer":0,"why":"Activation points pip at the venv's own site-packages folder."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"commands = [\"python -m venv .venv\", \"source .venv/bin/activate\", \"pip install requests\"]\nprint(len(commands))\nprint(commands[0])","options":["2\npython -m venv .venv","3\npython -m venv .venv","3\nsource .venv/bin/activate","1\npip install requests"],"answer":1,"hint":"Count the setup steps, then take the first one."}},{"id":"i87","section":"power-lab","track":"intermediate","title":"Type Hints in Practice","explain":"Type hints are notes on your functions — def add(a: int, b: int) -> int — that say what types you expect. Python ignores them at runtime, but they make code self-documenting and let tools like mypy catch mistakes before you run it.","takeaways":["`def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:` hints the argument and return types.","Hints do NOT enforce types at runtime — wrong types still run.","Run `mypy` to check hints statically; read `f.__annotations__` to see them."],"code":"def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n\ndef greet(name: str, excited: bool = False) -> str:\n    return \"Hi \" + name + (\"!\" if excited else \"\")\n\nprint(add(2, 3))\nprint(greet(\"Ava\", True))\nprint(add.__annotations__)","quiz":[{"q":"Do type hints stop you passing the wrong type at runtime?","options":["Yes, Python raises TypeError","No — they are notes; Python still runs the code","Only for ints","Only when mypy is installed"],"answer":1,"why":"Hints are ignored at runtime; only a static checker like mypy enforces them."},{"q":"In def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:, what does -> int mean?","options":["The function returns an int","The function takes one int","It converts the result to int","It is a comment"],"answer":0,"why":"The arrow annotates the return type."},{"q":"Which tool checks type hints without running your code?","options":["pip","mypy","venv","pdb"],"answer":1,"why":"mypy reads your hints and reports type mistakes statically."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def double(n: int) -> int:\n    return n * 2\n\nprint(double(21))\nprint(double(\"21\"))","options":["42\n2121","42\n42","Error","2121\n42"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hints are only notes — Python still runs the code, and a string times 2 repeats it."}},{"id":"a1","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"List Comprehensions & Lambdas","explain":"Comprehensions build lists in one expressive line — [expr for x in items if cond]. They're faster to read and often faster to run than manual loops. Lambdas are tiny anonymous functions, perfect for short transformations you pass to sorted(), map() or filter().","takeaways":["[x**2 for x in range(5)] builds a list in a single line.","Add if to filter: [x for x in items if x > 0].","lambda args: expr creates a throwaway one-line function."],"code":"squares = [x**2 for x in range(1, 11)]\nprint(\"Squares:\", squares)\n\nevens = [x for x in range(20) if x % 2 == 0]\nprint(\"Evens:\", evens)\n\nnums = [5, 2, 9, 1]\nprint(\"Sorted desc:\", sorted(nums, key=lambda x: -x))","quiz":[{"q":"[x**2 for x in range(3)] equals…","options":["[0, 1, 4]","[1, 4, 9]","[0, 1, 2]","Error"],"answer":0,"why":"range(3) gives 0, 1, 2 — and their squares are 0, 1, 4."},{"q":"A lambda is…","options":["a named function","an anonymous one-line function","a loop","a module"],"answer":1,"why":"lambda creates a small anonymous function, e.g. lambda x: x * 2."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint([n * 2 for n in nums if n % 2 == 0])","options":["[2, 4, 6, 8]","[4, 8]","[2, 4]","[4]"],"answer":1,"hint":"Filter keeps 2 and 4, then doubles each → [4, 8]."}},{"id":"a2","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Generators & yield","explain":"Generators produce values lazily — one at a time, using almost no memory. yield pauses the function, hands out a value, and resumes where it left off when asked for the next one. Perfect for huge or infinite sequences that would never fit in a list.","takeaways":["yield turns a function into a generator that produces values on demand.","Generators are memory-efficient: items are made one at a time.","(expr for x in items) is a generator expression — like a comprehension but lazy."],"code":"def fibonacci(n):\n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        yield a\n        a, b = b, a + b\n\nprint(list(fibonacci(10)))\n\n# Memory-friendly: squares of 1M numbers, one at a time\ngen = (x**2 for x in range(1_000_000))\nprint(\"First 3:\", [next(gen) for _ in range(3)])","quiz":[{"q":"What does yield do?","options":["Returns and destroys the function","Pauses, producing one value lazily","Prints a value","Raises an error"],"answer":1,"why":"yield hands out a value and freezes the function's state until the next value is requested."},{"q":"Why use a generator for a million items?","options":["Faster CPU","Almost no memory use","Shorter code","Better syntax"],"answer":1,"why":"Items are produced one at a time instead of building a giant list in memory."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\ng = gen()\nprint(next(g))","options":["1","2","[1, 2]","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"next() resumes the generator up to the first yield → 1."}},{"id":"a3","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Decorators","explain":"Decorators wrap functions to add behaviour — timing, logging, caching, access control — without touching the function's own code. The @ syntax applies a decorator cleanly. Under the hood, @timer on f is just f = timer(f).","takeaways":["@decorator above def applies wrapper logic around the function.","The wrapper uses *args, **kwargs to accept any arguments.","@wraps(func) preserves the original function's name and docstring."],"code":"import time\nfrom functools import wraps\n\ndef timer(func):\n    @wraps(func)\n    def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.time()\n        result = func(*args, **kwargs)\n        print(f\"{func.__name__} took {time.time()-start:.4f}s\")\n        return result\n    return wrapper\n\n@timer\ndef slow_add(a, b):\n    time.sleep(0.2)\n    return a + b\n\nprint(slow_add(3, 4))","quiz":[{"q":"What does @timer above def slow_add do?","options":["Deletes the function","Wraps it to add timing behaviour","Renames it","Runs it twice"],"answer":1,"why":"The decorator replaces slow_add with wrapper, which times the call then returns the result."},{"q":"Why use @wraps(func) inside a decorator?","options":["Speed","Preserves the original function's name/docstring","Required by Python","Encrypts the code"],"answer":1,"why":"Without it, the wrapped function would report the wrapper's name instead of the original."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def shout(fn):\n    def wrapper():\n        return fn().upper()\n    return wrapper\n\n@shout\ndef greet():\n    return \"hello\"\n\nprint(greet())","options":["hello","HELLO","None","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"@shout replaces greet with wrapper, which uppercases the result."}},{"id":"a4","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Modules, pip & Virtual Environments","explain":"Modules organise code into importable files. pip installs third-party packages from PyPI. Virtual environments (venv) give each project its own isolated set of packages so versions never clash. This trio is the professional Python workflow — learn it now and every future project gets easier.","takeaways":["import brings in stdlib or third-party modules; pip installs them.","python -m venv venv creates an isolated environment per project.","pip freeze > requirements.txt locks exact versions for sharing."],"code":"# mymath.py  →  import mymath\n# def square(x): return x * x\n\n# In your terminal:\n#   python -m venv venv        # create isolated environment\n#   source venv/bin/activate   # activate it\n#   pip install requests       # install a package\n#   pip freeze > requirements.txt   # lock dependencies\n\nimport math, random\nprint(\"π ≈\", round(math.pi, 4))\nprint(\"Lucky number:\", random.randint(1, 100))","quiz":[{"q":"What does pip install requests do?","options":["Runs a program","Downloads a third-party package","Creates a venv","Deletes the cache"],"answer":1,"why":"pip is Python's package installer; it fetches packages from PyPI."},{"q":"Why use a virtual environment?","options":["Faster internet","Isolate each project's dependencies","Required for print()","Smaller files"],"answer":1,"why":"Each project gets its own package versions — no conflicts between projects."}],"challenge":{"type":"blank","prompt":"Fill the blank to create a virtual environment named venv:","code":"python -m ___ venv","answer":["venv"],"hint":"The stdlib module for virtual environments is called venv."}},{"id":"a6","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Decorators in Practice","explain":"A decorator is a function that wraps another function to add behavior — logging, timing, access checks — without touching its code. The @ syntax is just shorthand for passing your function through the decorator. Once you see the pattern, you will spot decorators everywhere in real Python projects.","takeaways":["@decorator above def is shorthand for func = decorator(func).","The wrapper function runs extra code before/after the original.","Decorators keep cross-cutting concerns (timing, logging) out of business logic."],"code":"def shout(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return func().upper()\n    return wrapper\n@shout\ndef greet():\n    return \"hello\"\nprint(greet())","quiz":[{"q":"What does @shout above \"def greet\" do?","options":["Deletes the function","Passes greet through the shout decorator","Runs greet twice","Converts greet to a class"],"answer":1,"why":"@shout is shorthand for greet = shout(greet)."},{"q":"What must a decorator return?","options":["A string","A new (usually wrapped) function","Nothing","A number"],"answer":1,"why":"The decorator returns the replacement function that gets called instead."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def star(func):\n    def wrapper():\n        return \"*\" + func() + \"*\"\n    return wrapper\n\n@star\ndef word():\n    return \"hi\"\nprint(word())","options":["hi","*hi*","**hi**","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"The wrapper adds one star on each side of the result."}},{"id":"a7","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Generators & Iterators","explain":"Generators produce values one at a time, pausing at each yield and resuming on demand. That laziness means you can work with huge or even infinite sequences without building them all in memory. for loops and next() both know how to pull values from a generator.","takeaways":["yield pauses a function and emits one value; next() resumes it.","Generators are lazy — values are produced only when asked for.","A generator is single-use: once exhausted, it stays exhausted."],"code":"def countdown(n):\n    while n > 0:\n        yield n\n        n = n - 1\n\nfor x in countdown(3):\n    print(x)","quiz":[{"q":"How is yield different from return?","options":["It is faster","It pauses the function and resumes later, producing a sequence","It ends the program","It deletes variables"],"answer":1,"why":"yield suspends the function state; the next call continues after it."},{"q":"Why are generators memory-friendly?","options":["They compress data","They produce one item at a time instead of building a whole list","They run on the GPU","They skip error checking"],"answer":1,"why":"Laziness means only one value exists in memory at a time."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\ng = gen()\nprint(next(g))\nprint(next(g))","options":["1 then 1","1\n2","2 then 2","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"Each next() call resumes where the generator paused."}},{"id":"a11","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Decorators with Arguments & functools.wraps","explain":"A decorator factory is a function that takes arguments and returns the real decorator. Inside it, @functools.wraps(fn) copies the wrapped function's name, docstring, and metadata onto the wrapper so debugging and docs keep working.","takeaways":["A decorator with arguments is really a factory: outer call takes args, inner function is the decorator.","Always apply @functools.wraps(fn) to your wrapper to preserve __name__ and __doc__.","The wrapper usually takes (*args, **kwargs) so it works with any signature."],"code":"import functools\n\ndef repeat(n):\n    def decorator(fn):\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        def wrapper(*a, **k):\n            return [fn(*a, **k) for _ in range(n)]\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\n@repeat(2)\ndef greet(name):\n    return \"hi \" + name\n\nprint(greet(\"Ava\"), greet.__name__)","quiz":[{"q":"What does @functools.wraps(fn) do inside a decorator?","options":["Copies __name__, __doc__ and other metadata from fn onto the wrapper","Makes the wrapper run faster","Prevents the decorator from being applied twice","Turns fn into a generator"],"answer":0,"why":"wraps updates the wrapper's metadata to look like the original function."},{"q":"In the example, what does repeat(2) return?","options":["A decorator: a function that takes fn and returns the wrapper","The number 2","The wrapped function's return value","A list of two functions"],"answer":0,"why":"repeat(2) returns decorator, which is then applied to greet as @repeat(2)."},{"q":"Without functools.wraps, what would greet.__name__ be after decoration?","options":["'wrapper'","'greet'","'repeat'","It would raise AttributeError"],"answer":0,"why":"Without wraps, the name of the replacement function (wrapper) leaks through."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def tag(name):\n    def deco(fn):\n        fn.label = name\n        return fn\n    return deco\n\n@tag(\"math\")\ndef add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add.label, add(2, 3))","options":["math 5","add 5","math 6","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"tag('math') runs first and returns deco; deco then receives add."}},{"id":"a12","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Concurrency I: Threading Basics","explain":"Threads let one program juggle several tasks that spend most of their time waiting, like downloads or file reads. You start them with threading.Thread, wait with join(), and protect shared data with a Lock.","takeaways":["Threads shine for I/O-bound work, not CPU-bound number crunching (see the GIL lesson).","Always join() every thread you start so the main program waits for them.","Guard shared mutable state with threading.Lock to avoid race conditions."],"code":"import threading\n\nresults = []\nlock = threading.Lock()\n\ndef work(n):\n    with lock:\n        results.append(n * n)\n\nthreads = [threading.Thread(target=work, args=(i,)) for i in range(5)]\nfor t in threads:\n    t.start()\nfor t in threads:\n    t.join()\nprint(sorted(results))","quiz":[{"q":"Why do we call t.join() on each thread?","options":["To wait until the thread finishes before the program continues","To terminate the thread immediately","To give the thread a higher priority","To duplicate the thread"],"answer":0,"why":"join() blocks the calling thread until that thread completes."},{"q":"What is threading.Lock() used for?","options":["Stopping two threads from mutating shared data at the same time","Locking the computer screen","Encrypting data in transit","Pausing the main thread forever"],"answer":0,"why":"Only one thread can hold the lock, so the critical section runs atomically."},{"q":"Threads in CPython are a poor fit for which workload?","options":["CPU-heavy computation, because of the GIL","Downloading many files","Reading many files from disk","Handling many network connections"],"answer":0,"why":"The GIL stops threads from running Python bytecode in parallel on multiple cores."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import threading\n\nnums = []\n\ndef add(x):\n    nums.append(x)\n\nts = [threading.Thread(target=add, args=(i,)) for i in range(3)]\nfor t in ts:\n    t.start()\nfor t in ts:\n    t.join()\nprint(sorted(nums))","options":["[0, 1, 2]","[0, 1, 2, 3]","[]","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"join() guarantees every thread finished appending before the print runs."}},{"id":"a13","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Concurrency II: asyncio Basics","explain":"asyncio runs many tasks on a single thread with an event loop. Coroutines declared with async def pause at await, letting other tasks run while they wait — no threads, no GIL fights.","takeaways":["Write coroutines with async def and pause them with await.","asyncio.gather() runs coroutines concurrently and returns results in order.","asyncio.run(main()) starts the event loop from normal synchronous code."],"code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return \"data:\" + name\n\nasync def main():\n    out = await asyncio.gather(fetch(\"a\"), fetch(\"b\"), fetch(\"c\"))\n    print(out)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","quiz":[{"q":"What does await do inside a coroutine?","options":["Pauses this coroutine so the event loop can run others until the awaited thing completes","Freezes the entire program","Starts a new operating-system thread","Cancels the coroutine"],"answer":0,"why":"await yields control back to the event loop instead of blocking."},{"q":"asyncio.gather(a(), b(), c()) returns results in which order?","options":["The order the coroutines were passed in","The order they finish","Random order","Reverse order"],"answer":0,"why":"gather preserves argument order regardless of completion order."},{"q":"Which call runs an async main() from regular code?","options":["asyncio.run(main())","main.start()","await main() at module top level","asyncio.thread(main())"],"answer":0,"why":"asyncio.run() creates the event loop, runs the coroutine, and closes the loop."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def double(x):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return x * 2\n\nasync def main():\n    print(await asyncio.gather(double(1), double(2), double(3)))\n\nasyncio.run(main())","options":["[2, 4, 6]","[6, 4, 2]","(2, 4, 6)","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"gather keeps the order of its arguments in the result list."}},{"id":"a14","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"The GIL & Multiprocessing","explain":"The Global Interpreter Lock lets only one thread execute Python bytecode at a time, so threads can't speed up CPU-bound code. The multiprocessing module sidesteps the GIL with separate processes, each with its own interpreter and memory.","takeaways":["The GIL means one thread runs Python bytecode at a time per process.","multiprocessing gives true parallelism for CPU-bound work via separate processes.","Processes don't share memory: data is pickled back and forth, so keep payloads small."],"code":"import multiprocessing\n\ndef square(n):\n    return n * n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with multiprocessing.Pool(2) as pool:\n        print(pool.map(square, [1, 2, 3, 4]))","quiz":[{"q":"What does the GIL actually prevent?","options":["Two threads executing Python bytecode truly in parallel","Two separate processes running at once","Importing C extensions","Using more than one CPU core for separate programs"],"answer":0,"why":"The GIL is per-process; separate processes each have their own."},{"q":"Why guard multiprocessing code with if __name__ == \"__main__\"?","options":["So child processes re-importing the module don't spawn more children","It makes the code run faster","It is required for every Python script","It disables the GIL"],"answer":0,"why":"On some platforms children re-import the module, which would re-execute the spawn code."},{"q":"How do processes exchange data compared to threads?","options":["They don't share memory; data is serialized (pickled) between them","They share all memory freely","Through threading.Lock","They cannot exchange data at all"],"answer":0,"why":"Each process has isolated memory, so arguments and results are pickled."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import multiprocessing\n\ndef cube(n):\n    return n ** 3\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with multiprocessing.Pool(2) as pool:\n        print(pool.map(cube, [1, 2, 3]))","options":["[1, 8, 27]","[1, 2, 3]","[3, 2, 1]","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pool.map applies the function to each item and preserves input order."}},{"id":"a16","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Generators: send(), Coroutines & Pipelines","explain":"Calling .send(value) on a generator feeds a value in as the result of the currently-paused yield expression. That turns generators into coroutines you can chain into lazy data-processing pipelines.","takeaways":["yield both produces a value outward and receives the next send() value inward.","Prime a coroutine with next(gen) before the first send().","Chain small generators so each stage lazily transforms the stream."],"code":"def averager():\n    total = 0.0\n    count = 0\n    while True:\n        x = yield total / count if count else 0.0\n        total += x\n        count += 1\n\navg = averager()\nnext(avg)\navg.send(10)\navg.send(20)\nprint(avg.send(30))","quiz":[{"q":"In 'x = yield 10', what does gen.send(99) do?","options":["Resumes the generator with x bound to 99","Yields 99 out to the caller","Restarts the generator from the top","Immediately raises StopIteration"],"answer":0,"why":"send() resumes at the paused yield and the expression evaluates to the sent value."},{"q":"Why call next(gen) before the first send()?","options":["The generator must run to its first yield before it can receive a value","It clears the generator's memory","send() only works after close()","It is optional style with no effect"],"answer":0,"why":"A just-created generator hasn't started; only None can be sent before the first yield."},{"q":"What do generator-based pipelines buy you?","options":["Lazy, memory-efficient stage-by-stage data processing","Faster CPU arithmetic","Automatic multithreading","Free disk persistence"],"answer":0,"why":"Each stage yields items on demand instead of building giant intermediate lists."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def doubler():\n    total = 0\n    while True:\n        x = yield total\n        total += x * 2\n\ng = doubler()\nnext(g)\nprint(g.send(5))\nprint(g.send(3))","options":["10\n16","5\n8","0\n10","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace it: after send(5), total is 0 + 5*2 = 10, which is then yielded."}},{"id":"a19","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"functools Toolkit: lru_cache, partial, reduce","explain":"functools packs three high-leverage helpers: lru_cache memoizes expensive pure functions, partial freezes some arguments of a function, and reduce folds an iterable into a single value.","takeaways":["@lru_cache(maxsize=None) memoizes pure functions: same args, instant answer.","partial(fn, a) returns a new callable with argument a pre-filled.","reduce(fn, iterable, start) folds left; pair it with the operator module for clean code."],"code":"from functools import lru_cache, reduce\nimport operator\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fib(n):\n    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(fib(10))\nprint(reduce(operator.mul, [1, 2, 3, 4], 1))","quiz":[{"q":"What does @lru_cache do?","options":["Caches return values so repeated calls with the same arguments are instant","Deletes old function definitions","Runs the function on multiple cores","Converts recursion into iteration"],"answer":0,"why":"It memoizes: a cache keyed by arguments stores past results."},{"q":"What does partial(int, base=2) return?","options":["A callable like int but with base pre-set to 2","The integer 2","A syntax error","A new class"],"answer":0,"why":"partial freezes arguments, returning a new callable awaiting the rest."},{"q":"reduce(operator.add, [1, 2, 3], 10) equals?","options":["16","6","10","12310"],"answer":0,"why":"Start at 10, then 10+1+2+3 = 16."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\ncalls = []\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef f(x):\n    calls.append(x)\n    return x * 10\n\nf(2)\nf(2)\nf(3)\nprint(len(calls), f(2))","options":["2 20","3 20","2 2","3 30"],"answer":0,"hint":"The second f(2) hits the cache, so the body runs only twice total."}},{"id":"a21","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Type Hints & Static Checking","explain":"Type hints like def f(x: int) -> int document intent and let tools like mypy catch bugs before runtime. Python itself ignores them completely — they're metadata for humans and checkers, not enforcement.","takeaways":["Hints are documentation plus tooling; Python never enforces them at runtime.","Use list[int], dict[str, int], and X | None for modern container and optional hints.","Run mypy or pyright in CI to catch type errors before they ship."],"code":"def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(2, 3))\nprint(add.__annotations__)","quiz":[{"q":"Does Python enforce type hints at runtime?","options":["No: they are ignored at runtime; tools like mypy check them statically","Yes: wrong types raise TypeError","Only for int hints","Only inside classes"],"answer":0,"why":"Hints live in __annotations__; nothing checks them unless you run a checker."},{"q":"What does add.__annotations__ hold?","options":["The parameter and return annotations as a dict","The function's source code","The types of local variables","Nothing: the attribute does not exist"],"answer":0,"why":"Annotations are stored in a plain dict on the function object."},{"q":"How do you hint 'a list of ints' on modern Python (3.9+)?","options":["list[int]","List<int>","int[]","Array[int]"],"answer":0,"why":"Builtin generics like list[int] work since Python 3.9."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    return a + b\n\nprint(add(\"x\", \"y\"))","options":["xy","Error","None","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hints are not enforced: 'x' + 'y' is perfectly valid string concatenation."}},{"id":"a22","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Logging Done Right","explain":"The logging module replaces print-debugging with leveled, configurable output. Get a logger per module with getLogger(__name__), log at the right level, and configure handlers and formatting once at startup.","takeaways":["Use logging.getLogger(__name__) per module instead of the root logger directly.","Levels ascend: DEBUG < INFO < WARNING < ERROR < CRITICAL.","Configure once with basicConfig or dictConfig; library code should never print()."],"code":"import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=\"%(levelname)s: %(message)s\")\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"app\")\nlog.debug(\"hidden\")\nlog.info(\"started\")\nlog.warning(\"careful\")","quiz":[{"q":"With level=logging.INFO, which call produces no output?","options":["log.debug('x')","log.info('x')","log.warning('x')","log.error('x')"],"answer":0,"why":"DEBUG is below the INFO threshold, so it is filtered out."},{"q":"Why prefer logging over print() for diagnostics?","options":["Levels, timestamps, and routing to files or handlers without code changes","It is faster to type","print() is deprecated","Logging uses less memory"],"answer":0,"why":"Logging separates the decision to record from where the record goes."},{"q":"What does logging.getLogger('app') return on the second call?","options":["The same logger object as the first call","A brand-new logger","None","It raises an error"],"answer":0,"why":"Loggers are cached by name in a registry; same name, same object."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"demo\")\nprint(log.name)\nprint(log.getEffectiveLevel() == logging.WARNING)","options":["demo\nTrue","demo\nFalse","root\nTrue","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fresh logger inherits the default WARNING level from the root logger."}},{"id":"a23","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Debugging with pdb","explain":"pdb is Python's built-in debugger: calling breakpoint() drops you into an interactive shell at that line. Step with n, continue with c, inspect values with p, and quit with q.","takeaways":["breakpoint() starts a pdb session at that line (Python 3.7+).","n runs the next line, c continues, p <expr> prints a value, q quits.","Use pp for pretty-printing and l to see surrounding source."],"code":"def divide(a, b):\n    # breakpoint()  <- drop in here: try p a, then n, then c\n    return a / b\n\nprint(divide(10, 2))","quiz":[{"q":"What does breakpoint() do?","options":["Opens an interactive pdb session at that line","Stops the program permanently","Deletes the enclosing function","Runs the test suite"],"answer":0,"why":"It invokes the built-in debugger right where it is called."},{"q":"In pdb, what does the 'n' command do?","options":["Executes the current line and moves to the next one","Creates a new breakpoint","Prints all local variables","Exits the debugger"],"answer":0,"why":"n means next: step over the current line."},{"q":"How do you inspect a variable's value inside pdb?","options":["p varname","print is the only way","show varname","echo varname"],"answer":0,"why":"p evaluates and prints the expression in the current frame."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"If you put breakpoint() on the s += x line, pdb would show s as 1, then 3, then 6. What does the program print?","code":"def total(xs):\n    s = 0\n    for x in xs:\n        s += x\n    return s\n\nprint(total([1, 2, 3]))","options":["6","5","3","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"The debugger only observes; the loop still sums 1 + 2 + 3."}},{"id":"a24","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Packaging: Ship Your Own pip Package","explain":"Shipping a package means a pyproject.toml describing your project plus your code in a package directory. pip install -e . installs it editable for development, and python -m build plus twine take it to PyPI.","takeaways":["pyproject.toml holds name, version, dependencies, and the build backend.","Keep importable code in a package directory with an __init__.py.","pip install -e . for local development; build with python -m build."],"code":"# myapp/__init__.py\n__version__ = \"1.0.0\"\n\ndef hello(name):\n    return \"Hello, \" + name + \"!\"\n\nprint(__version__)\nprint(hello(\"pip\"))","quiz":[{"q":"What does pyproject.toml describe?","options":["Project metadata: name, version, dependencies, build system","Only the Python interpreter version","Your IDE settings","Git history"],"answer":0,"why":"It is the standard manifest pip and build tools read."},{"q":"What does pip install -e . do?","options":["Installs the local package in editable mode so changes take effect immediately","Erases the installed package","Installs from the internet only","Encrypts your source code"],"answer":0,"why":"Editable installs link to your working directory instead of copying."},{"q":"Where does importable package code live?","options":["In a directory with an __init__.py (or a namespace-package directory)","Only inside site-packages","Inside pyproject.toml itself","In any .txt file"],"answer":0,"why":"The package directory is what Python puts on the import path."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"__version__ = \"2.5.0\"\nmajor, minor, patch = __version__.split(\".\")\nprint(major, int(minor) + int(patch))","options":["2 5","2.5.0 5","2 7","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"split('.') gives ['2', '5', '0']; add the last two as integers."}},{"id":"a28","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Profiling & Optimization","explain":"Profile before you optimize: cProfile shows where your program really spends time, and timeit gives trustworthy micro-benchmarks. Most real wins come from better algorithms, not micro-tweaks.","takeaways":["cProfile plus pstats finds the actual hotspots: measure, don't guess.","timeit.repeat avoids one-off timing noise for fair comparisons.","Optimize algorithms first; readability beats cleverness everywhere else."],"code":"import cProfile, pstats, io\n\ndef slow():\n    return sum(i * i for i in range(10000))\n\npr = cProfile.Profile()\npr.enable()\nslow()\npr.disable()\nout = io.StringIO()\npstats.Stats(pr, stream=out).print_stats(1)\nprint(\"profiled ok\")","quiz":[{"q":"What is cProfile for?","options":["Measuring where your program spends its time","Checking code style","Compressing files","Encrypting data"],"answer":0,"why":"It records per-function call counts and cumulative time."},{"q":"Why use timeit instead of time.time() around code once?","options":["It repeats the measurement and reduces timing noise for stable results","It is shorter to type","time.time() is deprecated","It runs the code in parallel"],"answer":0,"why":"Repetition plus setup control makes micro-benchmarks trustworthy."},{"q":"What is the first rule of optimization?","options":["Measure first: profile before changing anything","Rewrite everything in C","Delete all comments","Use shorter variable names"],"answer":0,"why":"Guessing hotspots wastes effort; profilers show the truth."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import timeit\nt1 = timeit.timeit('\"-\".join(str(n) for n in range(100))', number=100)\nt2 = timeit.timeit('\"-\".join(map(str, range(100)))', number=100)\nprint(t1 > 0 and t2 > 0)","options":["True","False","0","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both timings are positive durations, so the comparison is always True."}},{"id":"a30","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Pythonic Idioms Masterclass","explain":"Pythonic code leans on the language: comprehensions, zip, enumerate, any/all, unpacking, and context managers. These idioms are shorter, often faster, and instantly readable to experienced Python developers.","takeaways":["Prefer comprehensions and any()/all() over manual accumulator loops.","Unpack with a, b = ... and a, *rest = ...; iterate pairs with zip and enumerate.","Idiomatic code is a feature: it signals intent to every Python reader."],"code":"words = [\"  Hello \", \"WORLD\", \" python \"]\nclean = [w.strip().lower() for w in words]\nprint(clean)\npairs = dict(zip([\"a\", \"b\"], [1, 2]))\nprint(pairs)\nprint(any(x > 5 for x in [1, 9, 3]))","quiz":[{"q":"Which is the most Pythonic way to build a doubled list?","options":["[x * 2 for x in xs]","A for loop with result.append(x * 2)","map() with a lambda stored in a variable","A while loop with a manual index"],"answer":0,"why":"The comprehension states the transformation directly and idiomatically."},{"q":"What does a, *rest = [1, 2, 3, 4] bind?","options":["a = 1, rest = [2, 3, 4]","a = [1], rest = [2, 3, 4]","It raises ValueError","a = 4, rest = [1, 2, 3]"],"answer":0,"why":"The starred target collects all remaining items into a list."},{"q":"Why is any(x > 5 for x in xs) better than a manual loop?","options":["It short-circuits on the first match and reads as pure intent","It is always measurably faster","for loops are deprecated","It only saves indentation"],"answer":0,"why":"any() stops early and the generator expresses the condition directly."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"data = [(\"a\", 1), (\"b\", 2), (\"c\", 3)]\nletters, nums = zip(*data)\nprint(letters)\nprint(sum(nums))","options":["('a', 'b', 'c')\n6","('a', 1)\n6","abc\n6","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"zip(*data) unzips: first elements together, second elements together."}},{"id":"a38","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Decorators: Building a Timer & Cache","explain":"A timer decorator wraps a function, records the start time, calls it, and reports how long it took. Pair that idea with functools.lru_cache and you get the two most common real-world decorators: measure it, then memoize it.","takeaways":["A timer decorator uses time.perf_counter() before and after the wrapped call.","Always use @functools.wraps(fn) so the wrapper keeps the original name and docstring.","@lru_cache(maxsize=None) memoizes pure functions: repeated calls with the same arguments return instantly."],"code":"import time\nimport functools\n\ndef timer(fn):\n    @functools.wraps(fn)\n    def wrapper(*a, **k):\n        t0 = time.perf_counter()\n        result = fn(*a, **k)\n        print(fn.__name__, \"took\", round(time.perf_counter() - t0, 6), \"s\")\n        return result\n    return wrapper\n\n@timer\ndef slow_sum(n):\n    return sum(range(n))\n\n@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fib(n):\n    return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)\n\nprint(slow_sum(100000))\nprint(fib(10))","quiz":[{"q":"Why use time.perf_counter() in a timer instead of time.time()?","options":["It returns the date","It pauses the function","It only works inside decorators","It is a monotonic high-resolution clock made for measuring durations"],"answer":3,"why":"perf_counter is designed for benchmarking: it never jumps backwards when the system clock changes."},{"q":"What does @lru_cache do on the second call fib(10)?","options":["Returns the cached result without re-running the body","Recomputes it from scratch","Raises an error","Returns None"],"answer":0,"why":"The cache is keyed by arguments, so a repeat call is a hit, not a recomputation."},{"q":"Why does the timer decorator apply @functools.wraps(fn) to the wrapper?","options":["To hide the wrapper from debuggers","To preserve the original function name and docstring","To make the function faster","To enable caching"],"answer":1,"why":"Without wraps, introspection sees the wrapper, not the real function."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\ncalls = []\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef double(x):\n    calls.append(x)\n    return x * 2\n\ndouble(3)\ndouble(3)\ndouble(4)\nprint(len(calls), double(3))","options":["3 8","2 6","3 6","2 3"],"answer":1,"hint":"The second double(3) is a cache hit, so the body runs only for 3 and 4."}},{"id":"a39","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Decorators: Retry & Rate Limiting","explain":"A retry decorator re-runs a flaky function a few times before giving up, which is how you survive a brief network hiccup. A rate-limit decorator does the opposite job: it slows calls down so you never exceed an API quota.","takeaways":["Retry only transient errors, and give up after a small number of attempts.","Add a short sleep between attempts so a struggling service can recover.","Rate limiting tracks the time of the last call and sleeps until the next one is allowed."],"code":"import functools\nimport time\n\ndef retry(times):\n    def decorator(fn):\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        def wrapper(*a, **k):\n            for attempt in range(times):\n                try:\n                    return fn(*a, **k)\n                except ValueError:\n                    if attempt == times - 1:\n                        raise\n                    time.sleep(0.01)\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\ntries = [0]\n\n@retry(3)\ndef fetch():\n    tries[0] += 1\n    if tries[0] < 3:\n        raise ValueError(\"flaky\")\n    return 42\n\nprint(fetch(), tries[0])","quiz":[{"q":"In the example, why does fetch() succeed on the third call?","options":["The retry decorator re-runs it until it stops raising ValueError","The exception is ignored silently","Python retries automatically","The function is cached"],"answer":0,"why":"retry(3) allows three attempts; the first two raise, the third returns 42."},{"q":"What should a retry decorator do after the final failed attempt?","options":["Restart the program","Re-raise the exception so the caller knows it failed","Return None silently","Retry forever"],"answer":1,"why":"Swallowing the error would hide a real failure; the caller needs to know."},{"q":"Why put a sleep between retry attempts?","options":["Sleep is required by Python","To reset the cache","To give the failing service a moment to recover","To make the code slower on purpose"],"answer":2,"why":"Hammering a struggling service instantly usually just fails again; a pause helps."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def retry(times):\n    def decorator(fn):\n        def wrapper(*a, **k):\n            for attempt in range(times):\n                try:\n                    return fn(*a, **k)\n                except ValueError:\n                    if attempt == times - 1:\n                        raise\n        return wrapper\n    return decorator\n\ncount = [0]\n\n@retry(2)\ndef flaky():\n    count[0] += 1\n    raise ValueError(\"nope\")\n\ntry:\n    flaky()\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"gave up after\", count[0])","options":["gave up after 3","Error","gave up after 2","gave up after 1"],"answer":2,"hint":"retry(2) allows two attempts, then the exception escapes."}},{"id":"a40","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Context Managers: @contextmanager","explain":"The @contextmanager decorator from contextlib turns a generator function into a context manager: code before yield is the setup, the yielded value is bound by as, and code after yield is the cleanup, which runs even if the block raises.","takeaways":["@contextmanager turns a generator into a with-ready object.","Code before yield sets up; code after yield cleans up, like __exit__.","Wrap the yield in try/finally so cleanup runs even when the block raises."],"code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef timer():\n    print(\"start\")\n    yield \"go\"\n    print(\"stop\")\n\nwith timer() as signal:\n    print(signal)","quiz":[{"q":"In a @contextmanager generator, what does the yield do?","options":["It deletes the context manager","It hands the value to the as-target and pauses until the block ends","It returns from the function permanently","It starts a new thread"],"answer":1,"why":"The generator pauses at yield; when the with block exits it resumes after yield for cleanup."},{"q":"Why wrap the yield in try/finally?","options":["To catch syntax errors","It is required for every generator","So cleanup runs even if the with block raises an exception","To make the code run faster"],"answer":2,"why":"An exception in the block is thrown into the generator at the yield; finally guarantees cleanup."},{"q":"What does @contextmanager save you from writing?","options":["An import statement","A decorator","A test file","A full class with __enter__ and __exit__"],"answer":3,"why":"The decorator builds the context manager protocol from a single generator function."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"from contextlib import contextmanager\n\n@contextmanager\ndef tag():\n    print(\"in\")\n    yield 5\n    print(\"out\")\n\nwith tag() as value:\n    print(value)","options":["5\nin\nout","in\nout\n5","Error","in\n5\nout"],"answer":3,"hint":"Setup prints first, then the block body, then the cleanup after yield."}},{"id":"a41","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Asyncio: Tasks & Gathering Results","explain":"asyncio.create_task() schedules a coroutine to run in the background while your code carries on, and await asyncio.gather() collects the results of several tasks at once. The results always come back in the order you passed the tasks, not the order they finished.","takeaways":["asyncio.create_task(coro) schedules a coroutine to run concurrently.","await asyncio.gather(*tasks) waits for all of them and returns results in argument order.","Cancel a task with task.cancel() when you no longer need its result."],"code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def work(name):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return name + \" done\"\n\nasync def main():\n    tasks = [asyncio.create_task(work(n)) for n in (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\")]\n    print(await asyncio.gather(*tasks))\n\nasyncio.run(main())","quiz":[{"q":"What does asyncio.create_task() do?","options":["Starts a new operating-system thread","Converts a coroutine into a normal function","Schedules a coroutine to run concurrently on the event loop","Runs the coroutine immediately and blocks"],"answer":2,"why":"It wraps the coroutine in a Task and schedules it; the loop runs it when it can."},{"q":"await asyncio.gather(t1, t2) returns results in which order?","options":["The order they finished","Random order","Reverse order","The order the tasks were passed in"],"answer":3,"why":"gather preserves argument order in its result list regardless of completion order."},{"q":"What happens if one task passed to gather raises?","options":["gather raises that exception (and by default the other tasks keep running)","It returns None for that task","It silently retries the task","The event loop crashes permanently"],"answer":0,"why":"By default gather propagates the first exception; pass return_exceptions=True to collect errors as results instead."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def square(n):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return n * n\n\nasync def main():\n    print(await asyncio.gather(square(2), square(3), square(4)))\n\nasyncio.run(main())","options":["[4, 9, 16]","[16, 9, 4]","(4, 9, 16)","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"gather returns a list in the order the coroutines were passed."}},{"id":"a42","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Asyncio: Async File & Network IO","explain":"Async code shines when waiting on files or the network, but you still need the right tool for each: use asyncio.to_thread for blocking file reads, and an async HTTP client for real requests. Here the network reply is sample data, exactly what an API would hand you, so nothing touches the real network.","takeaways":["asyncio.to_thread() runs blocking calls like open() without freezing the event loop.","Never call requests.get inside a coroutine: it blocks the whole loop.","Parse the response with json.loads whether it came from a real API or sample data."],"code":"import asyncio\nimport json\n\nsample = \"{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Ava\\\", \\\"score\\\": 92}\"\n\nasync def fetch_user():\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return json.loads(sample)\n\ndef _read(path):\n    with open(path) as f:\n        return f.read()\n\nasync def main():\n    with open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(\"hello async\")\n    user = await fetch_user()\n    text = await asyncio.to_thread(_read, \"notes.txt\")\n    print(user[\"name\"], user[\"score\"], text)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","quiz":[{"q":"Why use asyncio.to_thread() for reading a file inside a coroutine?","options":["Files cannot be opened in async code at all","It makes the file larger","It encrypts the file","open() blocks, and to_thread runs it off the event loop so other tasks keep moving"],"answer":3,"why":"A blocking call inside a coroutine would freeze the whole loop; to_thread moves it to a worker thread."},{"q":"Why does this lesson parse sample JSON instead of calling a real API?","options":["So the code runs offline and deterministically, exactly as a real response would look","Real APIs never return JSON","json.loads only works on sample data","Asyncio forbids network access"],"answer":0,"why":"The sample string is shaped like a real API response, so the parsing code is identical but needs no network."},{"q":"What would a blocking requests.get() do inside a coroutine?","options":["Speed up the other tasks","Freeze the entire event loop until the response arrives","Run in parallel automatically","Raise a syntax error"],"answer":1,"why":"The loop is single-threaded: a blocking call stalls every task, not just the current one."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import asyncio\nimport json\n\nasync def fake_get():\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return json.loads(\"{\\\"city\\\": \\\"Pune\\\", \\\"temp\\\": 28}\")\n\nasync def main():\n    data = await fake_get()\n    print(data[\"city\"], data[\"temp\"])\n\nasyncio.run(main())","options":["Error","Pune 28","Pune 0","28 Pune"],"answer":1,"hint":"fake_get returns the parsed sample dict; the prints just read its keys."}},{"id":"a43","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Threading: Locks & Race Conditions","explain":"A race condition happens when two threads read and write the same value at the same time, so updates get lost. A threading.Lock makes the read-modify-write step atomic: only the thread holding the lock can be inside that block, so no update is ever lost.","takeaways":["A race condition is lost or corrupted updates from unsynchronised shared access.","Wrap the critical section in with lock: so only one thread runs it at a time.","Keep the locked section as small as possible so threads are not waiting on each other."],"code":"import threading\n\ncounter = [0]\nlock = threading.Lock()\n\ndef inc():\n    for _ in range(1000):\n        with lock:\n            counter[0] += 1\n\nthreads = [threading.Thread(target=inc) for _ in range(4)]\nfor t in threads:\n    t.start()\nfor t in threads:\n    t.join()\nprint(counter[0])","quiz":[{"q":"Why does the example print exactly 4000 every time?","options":["The lock makes each increment atomic, so no update is lost","Threads always run in order","Python integers are thread-safe by themselves","join() re-runs any lost increments"],"answer":0,"why":"With the lock, the read-add-write of counter[0] cannot be interleaved with another thread."},{"q":"What is a race condition?","options":["A lock that never unlocks","Two threads interleaving access to shared data so the result depends on timing","Two threads racing to finish first","A thread that runs too fast"],"answer":1,"why":"The outcome depends on the unpredictable interleaving of the threads."},{"q":"What happens if a thread forgets to release a lock it acquired?","options":["The program runs faster","Python raises an error immediately","Other threads block forever waiting for it (deadlock)","The lock releases itself after one second"],"answer":2,"why":"A lock stays held until released, so a missing release stalls every waiter; use with lock: to avoid it."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import threading\n\ntotal = [0]\nlock = threading.Lock()\n\ndef add(n):\n    with lock:\n        total[0] += n\n\nts = [threading.Thread(target=add, args=(5,)) for _ in range(3)]\nfor t in ts:\n    t.start()\nfor t in ts:\n    t.join()\nprint(total[0])","options":["0","Error","15","5"],"answer":2,"hint":"Three threads each add 5 under the lock, and join waits for all of them."}},{"id":"a44","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Multiprocessing: Pools & Queues","explain":"A multiprocessing Pool spreads CPU-heavy work across processes, each with its own interpreter, so the GIL cannot slow you down. A Queue is how those separate processes send results back: a worker puts values in, and the main process gets them out.","takeaways":["Pool.map(fn, items) runs fn on each item across worker processes and keeps input order.","Processes do not share memory: results travel back through a Queue (pickled).","Always close or context-manage the pool and join() processes so nothing is left running."],"code":"import multiprocessing\n\ndef square(n):\n    return n * n\n\ndef put(q, v):\n    q.put(v * v)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with multiprocessing.Pool(2) as pool:\n        print(pool.map(square, [1, 2, 3]))\n    q = multiprocessing.Queue()\n    ps = [multiprocessing.Process(target=put, args=(q, i)) for i in (4, 5)]\n    for p in ps:\n        p.start()\n    print(sorted(q.get() for _ in ps))\n    for p in ps:\n        p.join()","quiz":[{"q":"Why use a Pool instead of threads for squaring a million numbers?","options":["Pools share memory automatically","Processes sidestep the GIL, so CPU work truly runs in parallel","Pools use less memory","Threads cannot call functions"],"answer":1,"why":"Each process has its own interpreter and GIL, so CPU-bound work scales across cores."},{"q":"How does a child process send a result back to the parent?","options":["By returning from the target function","Through threading.Lock","Through a Queue (or the Pool return value); processes do not share memory","By writing to a shared global variable"],"answer":2,"why":"Memory is isolated per process, so results must be pickled through a Queue or collected by the Pool."},{"q":"Why guard the pool code with if __name__ == __main__?","options":["It makes the pool faster","It is required for every Python file","It disables the GIL","So child processes re-importing the module do not spawn more children"],"answer":3,"why":"On spawn-based platforms children re-import the module, which would re-run the pool creation."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import multiprocessing\n\ndef double(n):\n    return n * 2\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    with multiprocessing.Pool(2) as pool:\n        print(pool.map(double, [3, 4]))","options":["[3, 4]","[8, 6]","Error","[6, 8]"],"answer":3,"hint":"Pool.map applies the function to each item and preserves input order."}},{"id":"a45","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Testing: pytest Fundamentals","explain":"pytest finds functions whose names start with test_ and runs them, reporting any failed assert as a failure. You do not need a test class or special methods: a plain function and a plain assert are a complete test, and you can run the same functions directly to see them pass.","takeaways":["Name test functions test_something so pytest discovers them.","Use plain assert statements: pytest shows exactly which values differed.","Run tests with pytest in the terminal; each function here also runs directly as a demo."],"code":"def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 3) == 5\n\ndef test_add_zero():\n    assert add(0, 0) == 0\n\ndef test_add_negative():\n    assert add(-1, 1) == 0\n\nfor t in (test_add, test_add_zero, test_add_negative):\n    t()\nprint(\"3 passed\")","quiz":[{"q":"How does pytest discover a test function?","options":["It returns True","It has a docstring","Its name starts with test_","It is defined in a class"],"answer":2,"why":"pytest collects functions and files matching the test_ naming pattern."},{"q":"What does pytest do when an assert fails inside a test?","options":["Skips the test silently","Deletes the test","Stops Python from running","Reports that test as FAILED and shows the values that differed"],"answer":3,"why":"A failed assert is the signal: pytest catches it and reports the mismatch."},{"q":"Why write several small tests instead of one big test?","options":["A failure points at exactly the broken behaviour instead of a vague block","pytest only runs small tests","Big tests run slower","Small tests need no asserts"],"answer":0,"why":"One behaviour per test makes failures precise and the suite easier to read."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def is_even(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0\n\ndef test_even():\n    assert is_even(4)\n\ndef test_odd():\n    assert not is_even(5)\n\nfor t in (test_even, test_odd):\n    t()\nprint(\"2 passed\")","options":["2 passed","0 passed","Error","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both asserts hold, so both test functions return without raising."}},{"id":"a46","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Testing: Fixtures & Parametrize","explain":"A fixture is a reusable setup helper that builds the fresh data each test needs, so tests never share state. Parametrize runs the same test once per input case, so one test function covers a whole table of examples instead of copy-pasting the same check.","takeaways":["A fixture builds fresh data per test; tests should never share mutable setup.","In pytest, @pytest.fixture marks the setup function and tests receive it by name.","@pytest.mark.parametrize runs one test over a list of (input, expected) cases."],"code":"def make_scores():\n    return [80, 90, 100]\n\ndef average(scores):\n    return sum(scores) / len(scores)\n\ndef test_average(scores, expected):\n    assert average(scores) == expected\n\ncases = [([10, 20], 15), ([0, 0], 0), ([5], 5)]\nfor scores, expected in cases:\n    test_average(scores, expected)\nprint(\"3 cases passed\")","quiz":[{"q":"What problem does a fixture solve?","options":["Tests run faster","Tests need no asserts","It replaces the code under test","Every test gets fresh setup data instead of sharing mutable state"],"answer":3,"why":"Shared setup leaks state between tests; a fixture rebuilds it per test."},{"q":"What does @pytest.mark.parametrize do?","options":["Runs the same test once for each case in a list","Runs the test in parallel","Skips the test","Generates random inputs"],"answer":0,"why":"Each parameter set becomes its own test run with its own pass or fail."},{"q":"In the example, how many times does test_average run?","options":["15 times","3 times, once per case","Once","0 times: it is only a helper"],"answer":1,"why":"The loop feeds the three cases in one by one, mirroring parametrize."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def double(x):\n    return x * 2\n\ncases = [(1, 2), (2, 4), (3, 6)]\nfor x, expected in cases:\n    assert double(x) == expected\nprint(len(cases), \"cases passed\")","options":["6 cases passed","3 cases passed","1 cases passed","Error"],"answer":1,"hint":"All three cases hold, and len(cases) is 3."}},{"id":"a47","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Testing: Mocking External Services","explain":"A mock is a fake object that stands in for a real service during a test, returning canned answers you control. You test your own logic, like how you handle a returned user, without ever calling the real API, database, or payment service.","takeaways":["Mock the boundary (API, database, network) and test your own logic against canned answers.","unittest.mock.Mock lets you set return_value and check call_count afterwards.","Pass the dependency in (dependency injection) so tests can swap in a mock."],"code":"from unittest.mock import Mock\n\ndef greet(client, user_id):\n    user = client.get_user(user_id)\n    return \"hi \" + user[\"name\"]\n\nfake = Mock()\nfake.get_user.return_value = {\"name\": \"Ava\"}\nprint(greet(fake, 7))\nprint(fake.get_user.call_count)","quiz":[{"q":"Why mock an external service in a test?","options":["So the test is fast, offline, and deterministic instead of depending on a live service","Mocks make the real service faster","Tests cannot call real code","Mocking is required by Python"],"answer":0,"why":"A live service can be slow, flaky, or charge money; a mock returns exactly what you set."},{"q":"What does fake.get_user.return_value = {...} do?","options":["Raises an error","Makes every call to fake.get_user() return that dict","Calls the real API once","Deletes the method"],"answer":1,"why":"return_value is the canned answer the mock hands back on every call."},{"q":"How can you check the mock was actually used?","options":["Check the network log","You cannot check it","Inspect fake.get_user.call_count or assert_called_once_with(...)","Print the mock object"],"answer":2,"why":"Mocks record every call, so you can assert your code called the service exactly as expected."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"from unittest.mock import Mock\n\ndef score_of(client, user_id):\n    return client.get_user(user_id)[\"score\"]\n\nfake = Mock()\nfake.get_user.return_value = {\"score\": 92}\nprint(score_of(fake, 1))\nprint(fake.get_user.call_count)","options":["None\n1","Error","92\n1","92\n0"],"answer":2,"hint":"The mock returns the canned dict, and it was called exactly once."}},{"id":"a48","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Testing: Coverage & CI Basics","explain":"Coverage measures which lines of your code your tests actually execute, so you can see the untested corners. Continuous integration runs the whole suite on every push, so a broken change is caught by the server instead of by your users.","takeaways":["Coverage reports the percentage of lines your tests execute; aim high on core logic, not 100% everywhere.","Run pytest --cov=. to generate a coverage report.","In CI, the pipeline installs dependencies, runs pytest, and fails the build on any failing test."],"code":"def total(prices):\n    return sum(prices)\n\ndef test_empty():\n    assert total([]) == 0\n\ndef test_some():\n    assert total([10, 20]) == 30\n\ndef test_one():\n    assert total([5]) == 5\n\ntests = [test_empty, test_some, test_one]\npassed = 0\nfor t in tests:\n    t()\n    passed += 1\nprint(passed, \"/\", len(tests), \"tests passed\")","quiz":[{"q":"What does code coverage measure?","options":["How large the codebase is","Which lines of code your tests actually execute","How fast the tests run","How many tests you wrote"],"answer":1,"why":"Coverage instruments the code and reports the executed lines versus the missed ones."},{"q":"What does a CI pipeline do on every push?","options":["Deletes old branches","Writes new tests for you","Installs dependencies and runs the test suite, failing the build on any failure","Deploys straight to production"],"answer":2,"why":"CI gives every change the same automated check before a human reviews it."},{"q":"Why is 100% coverage a weak goal on its own?","options":["It is impossible to reach","Coverage tools are inaccurate","It slows the tests down","It measures lines executed, not whether the asserts actually check the right behaviour"],"answer":3,"why":"A test can execute a line without asserting anything useful about it."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"def double(x):\n    return x * 2\n\ntests = [lambda: double(2), lambda: double(3)]\npassed = 0\nfor t in tests:\n    assert t() == 4 or t() == 6\n    passed += 1\nprint(passed, \"/\", len(tests), \"tests passed\")","options":["1 / 2 tests passed","0 / 2 tests passed","Error","2 / 2 tests passed"],"answer":3,"hint":"double(2) is 4, so the first assert passes at once; double(3) is 6, so the second assert passes via the or's second check (t() == 6)."}},{"id":"a76","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Packaging: Wheels, pyproject & Publishing","explain":"Packaging turns your code into something others can install with pip. A pyproject.toml file declares the name, version and dependencies, and building it produces a wheel — a ready-to-install .whl file you can upload to PyPI.","takeaways":["`pyproject.toml` is the modern home of package metadata: name, version, and dependencies.","A wheel filename looks like `mytool-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl`: name, version, Python tag, platform tag.","`python -m build` builds the wheel; `twine upload` publishes it to PyPI."],"code":"project = {\"name\": \"skillzify-tools\", \"version\": \"0.1.0\", \"requires-python\": \">=3.10\"}\nprint(project[\"name\"], project[\"version\"])\nwheel = project[\"name\"].replace(\"-\", \"_\") + \"-\" + project[\"version\"] + \"-py3-none-any.whl\"\nprint(\"wheel name:\", wheel)","quiz":[{"q":"What is pyproject.toml for?","options":["Declaring a package name, version, and dependencies for building","Storing your source code","Running your test suite","Logging errors at runtime"],"answer":0,"why":"Build tools read pyproject.toml to know what they are packaging."},{"q":"What is a wheel (.whl) file?","options":["A compressed backup of your project","A test report","A pre-built package archive that pip can install directly","A Python source file"],"answer":2,"why":"Wheels are the installable format pip downloads from PyPI."},{"q":"In the wheel name mytool-0.1.0-py3-none-any.whl, what is 0.1.0?","options":["The package version","The Python version","The file size","The upload date"],"answer":0,"why":"Wheel names follow name-version-python-abi-platform, so 0.1.0 is the version."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"project = {\"name\": \"quiz-app\", \"version\": \"2.3.1\"}\nprint(project[\"name\"] + \" \" + project[\"version\"])","options":["quiz-app 2.3.1","quiz_app 2.3.1","quiz-app2.3.1","Error"],"answer":0,"hint":"The plus joins the two strings with the space in between."}},{"id":"a77","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Profiling: Finding Bottlenecks","explain":"Profiling means measuring where your program actually spends its time instead of guessing. A one-line timing test can prove that a set lookup beats a list scan by a hundred times — and that is the kind of fact you optimize on.","takeaways":["`timeit.timeit(code, setup=..., number=1000)` times a snippet fairly by repeating it.","Set and dict lookups are near-instant even at 10,000 items; list scans grow with size.","Profile first, optimize second: measure the real hotspot before rewriting code."],"code":"import timeit\n\nlist_time = timeit.timeit(\"9999 in xs\", setup=\"xs = list(range(10000))\", number=1000)\nset_time = timeit.timeit(\"9999 in xs\", setup=\"xs = set(range(10000))\", number=1000)\nprint(\"set faster:\", set_time < list_time)\nprint(list_time > 0 and set_time > 0)","quiz":[{"q":"Why use timeit instead of timing code once with time.time()?","options":["time.time() is deprecated","Repeating the measurement averages out noise and gives a stable result","time.time() cannot time loops","timeit runs the code in parallel"],"answer":1,"why":"One run is noisy; hundreds of repeats reveal the real difference."},{"q":"Why is 9999 in a set of 10,000 items so much faster than in a list?","options":["Sets store items in sorted order","Lists check every item twice","Sets use less memory","Set lookup uses a hash and is roughly constant time"],"answer":3,"why":"Hashing jumps straight to the item instead of scanning from the start."},{"q":"What is the first step when code feels slow?","options":["Add more threads","Measure with a profiler or timeit to find the real hotspot","Rewrite the whole program in another language","Delete all comments"],"answer":1,"why":"Guessing wastes effort; measurement shows where the time actually goes."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import timeit\nt = timeit.timeit(\"[x * x for x in range(100)]\", number=100)\nprint(t > 0)","options":["Error","True","False","0"],"answer":1,"hint":"A timing is always a positive number of seconds."}},{"id":"a78","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Caching Strategies: lru_cache & Redis Intro","explain":"Caching means remembering an answer instead of recomputing it. functools.lru_cache remembers function results in memory, while Redis is a separate in-memory server that many processes — or many machines — can share, with keys that expire automatically.","takeaways":["`@lru_cache(maxsize=128)` memoizes a function: repeated calls with the same arguments return instantly.","`fn.cache_info()` shows hits, misses, and size so you can prove the cache is working.","Redis is a shared in-memory key-value store with expiry (TTL); use it when the cache must outlive one process."],"code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\ncalls = []\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=128)\ndef square(n):\n    calls.append(n)\n    return n * n\n\nprint(square(5), square(5), square(6))\nprint(\"computed:\", len(calls))\nprint(square.cache_info().hits)","quiz":[{"q":"What does @lru_cache do to a function?","options":["Deletes the function after one call","Saves results to a database file","Remembers past results so repeated calls with the same arguments skip the work","Makes the function run in parallel"],"answer":2,"why":"The cache stores argument-to-result pairs and replays them on a hit."},{"q":"In the example, why is len(calls) 2 after three calls?","options":["The second square(5) was a cache hit, so the function body ran only twice","square(6) was skipped","lru_cache drops the first call","calls only records even numbers"],"answer":0,"why":"square(5) computed once, then hit the cache; square(6) computed once."},{"q":"When would you pick Redis over lru_cache?","options":["When you only cache one value","When you want the slowest possible cache","When the cache must be shared across processes or machines and survive restarts","When the function has no arguments"],"answer":2,"why":"lru_cache lives inside one process; Redis is a shared server with expiry."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=10)\ndef double(n):\n    return n * 2\n\nprint(double(3), double(3))\nprint(double.cache_info().hits)","options":["6 6\n0","6 3\n1","Error","6 6\n1"],"answer":3,"hint":"The second call with 3 is a hit, not a recomputation."}},{"id":"a81","section":"power-lab","track":"advanced","title":"Code Quality: Linting with Ruff & Black","explain":"Linting catches bugs before they run: Ruff flags unused imports, undefined names, and style slips in milliseconds, while Black reformats your code to one consistent style. Together they make every file look like it was written by the same careful developer.","takeaways":["Ruff is a linter: it flags unused imports, undefined names, and style problems without running your code.","Black is a formatter: it rewrites spacing and line breaks to one standard style.","Run both in CI so messy code never merges; `py_compile` is a quick syntax sanity check."],"code":"import py_compile, os\n\nopen(\"lint_demo.py\", \"w\").write(\"x = 1\\nprint(x + 1)\\n\")\npy_compile.compile(\"lint_demo.py\", doraise=True)\nprint(\"compiles ok:\", os.path.exists(\"lint_demo.py\"))\nprint(\"ruff would flag: unused import\")\nprint(\"black would format: x=1  ->  x = 1\")","quiz":[{"q":"What does a linter like Ruff do?","options":["Deletes unused files","Flags likely bugs and style problems without running the code","Runs your program faster","Formats code automatically"],"answer":1,"why":"Linting is static analysis: it reads the code and reports problems."},{"q":"What is Black for?","options":["Finding security vulnerabilities","Running unit tests","Installing packages","Reformatting code to one consistent style"],"answer":3,"why":"Black only changes formatting, never behaviour."},{"q":"Why run Ruff and Black in CI (continuous integration)?","options":["CI replaces the need to write tests","So style and lint problems are caught on every push before they merge","So tests run slower","So code is hidden from reviewers"],"answer":1,"why":"Automated checks enforce quality on every change, not just when someone remembers."}],"challenge":{"type":"predict","prompt":"What will this print?","code":"import py_compile\nopen(\"ok.py\", \"w\").write(\"a = 2\\nprint(a)\\n\")\npy_compile.compile(\"ok.py\", doraise=True)\nprint(\"ok\")","options":["ok","Error","None","2"],"answer":0,"hint":"doraise only raises on a syntax error, and this file is valid."}}];
